---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import DocCard from '../../../components/DocCard.astro';
const sidebar = [
  { title: 'Documentation Home', href: '/docs' },
  { title: 'Operations Overview', href: '/docs/operations' },
  { title: 'Two-Host', href: '/docs/operations/two-host', active: true },
  { title: 'Single-Host', href: '/docs/operations/single-host' },
  { title: 'Hardening', href: '/docs/operations/hardening' },
  { title: 'Runbooks', href: '/docs/operations/runbooks' },
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'ids', text: 'Dashboard IDs (Edge)', level: 2 },
  { id: 'split', text: 'SIEM Two-Host Split', level: 2 },
  { id: 'topology', text: 'Two-Host Topology', level: 2 },
  { id: 'responsibilities', text: 'Host Responsibilities', level: 2 },
  { id: 'verify', text: 'Verify the Split', level: 2 },
];
---
<DocsLayout title="Two-Host Deployment" description="Run the edge stack and the SIEM stack on separate hosts" section="Operations" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    The two-host shape is the intended production layout. The edge host is internet-facing and holds the
    only components that see client requests. The SIEM host stores and visualises telemetry, and it never
    initiates a connection into the edge network.
  </p>
  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    <DocCard title="Edge" icon="server" tag="Internet-facing" description="Reverse proxy, production shop, honeypot pools, Redis, Suricata and a local Vector shipper." meta="Request path" />
    <DocCard title="SIEM" icon="chart" tag="Internal" description="Vector aggregator, Elasticsearch and Kibana. Receives logs over mTLS and serves dashboards to analysts." meta="Storage + views" />
    <DocCard title="Channel" icon="lock" tag="Outbound only" description="The edge opens the connection. No inbound path from the SIEM into the edge is required for logging." meta="mTLS" />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="ids">Dashboard IDs (Edge)</h2>
  <p><strong>Alerts & Overview:</strong> dashboard-ids-alerts, dashboard-web-threat-overview</p>
  <p><strong>Threat Intelligence & Analysis:</strong> dashboard-threat-decisions, dashboard-session-analysis, dashboard-attack-patterns, dashboard-threat-intel</p>

  <h2 id="split">SIEM Two-Host Split</h2>
  <p>Dashboards are split by functional domain:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Operational View:</strong> Real-time alerts and web traffic overview.</li>
    <li><strong>Strategic View:</strong> Deep threat analysis, decision-making, and intelligence.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="topology">Two-Host Topology</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Edge Host:</strong> Runs the reverse proxy, honeypot backend, and dashboard/monitoring components. The Nginx Lua pipeline (pattern_analyzer, router, pool_router) runs here.</li>
    <li><strong>SIEM Host:</strong> Runs Kibana/Elasticsearch and consumes logs from the edge host via Vector over mTLS.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Security events (SECURITY_EVENT: JSON in Nginx error log) are shipped by Vector to the SIEM host's Elasticsearch. Suricata on the edge host writes eve.json alerts, ingested by init_worker.lua into the threat_intel shared dict.</p>
  <h3>Setting up the mTLS channel</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>On the SIEM host, run <code>siem/certs/root-ca/gen_elk_certs.sh</code> to create the CA and sign certificates for <code>es01</code>, <code>kibana</code>, <code>vector</code> and <code>vector-agent</code>.</li>
    <li>Copy the generated <code>vector-agent</code> client certificate (<code>siem/vector/certs/vector-agent/</code>) to the edge host's <code>ids/vector/certs/</code> before starting the edge shipper.</li>
    <li>Point the edge shipper at the SIEM with <code>VECTOR_HOST</code> (a raw IP in production). The <code>vector</code> server certificate's Subject Alternative Name list covers connecting by IP as well as by hostname.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Details of the index naming and the VRL parse step are on the <a href="/docs/observability/siem#transport">SIEM &amp; Log Pipeline</a> page.</p>

  <h2 id="responsibilities">Host Responsibilities</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Capability</th><th>Edge host</th><th>SIEM host</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Client-facing TLS</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Request scoring and routing</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><td>WordPress production + pools</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Session state (Redis)</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Suricata capture</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Log aggregation</td><td>Local Vector shipper</td><td>Vector aggregator</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Indexing and dashboards</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 id="verify">Verify the Split</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Confirm the edge can start with the SIEM offline; requests must still be scored and routed.</li>
    <li>Confirm events appear in Kibana after the SIEM returns, including events buffered during the outage.</li>
    <li>Confirm the SIEM cannot reach the edge's private services; only the outbound log channel is required.</li>
    <li>Generate a known pattern from the edge and follow its decision trace into the corresponding Kibana document.</li>
  </ol>
</DocsLayout>
